    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d251">

                <pb facs="0309" n="211" sameAs="frus:frus1930v02/pg211"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">393.11/1316: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Canton</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Ballantine</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Canton</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1930-11-07T13:00:00+08:00">November 7, 1930—1
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received November 8—6:24 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>Referring to my telegram of October 29, 4 p.m.,<anchor n="12" xml:id="d251-f1"/>
                    Canton Municipal Government has today received a telegram from the Chairman of
                    the Kiangsi Provincial Government stating that instructions have been issued to
                    appropriate military and civil authorities to provide an adequate escort to
                    Kwangtung for Americans in Kanchow, who are now willing to evacuate.</p>
                <p>At my suggestion, municipal office is arranging to have instruction sent by
                    Acting Chairman of the Provincial Government to local authorities to provide
                    adequate escort for the party from the Kwangtung border to the rail head at
                    Shiuchow. Department and Legation and Hankow informed.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Ballantine</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
